// @run
// @log 5
// @log ab cd
// @log 4
// @log abcd
// @log 6
// @log 9
// @log 32
// STRING LINE CONTINUATION — a backslash immediately before a newline drops BOTH, and
// the LEXER and the EMITTER must agree about that.
//
// WHY THIS FIXTURE EXISTS. VL decodes a quoted lexeme TWICE, in two different files:
// `lexer.scanQuoted` (lexer.vl) resolves escapes while scanning, and `emit_base.decodeStr`
// resolves them again from the raw lexeme at emit time. `decodeStr`'s header promises it
// resolves escapes "consistently with the lexer's `scanQuoted`" — and for the line
// continuation it did not. The lexer had the arm; `decodeStr` did not, so the sequence fell
// through to the unknown-escape tail (keep the char after the backslash verbatim) and the
// newline SURVIVED. `"ab\<newline> cd"` was the 5-char `ab cd` to the lexer and the 6-char
// `ab<newline> cd` to the emitter. `vl check` was clean; nothing in the corpus looked.
//
// Two decoders for one grammar is the standing hazard here, and the failure mode is quiet:
// the program runs, prints something plausible, and is simply a different string than the
// one the source denotes. Every arm added to either decoder needs its twin in the other.

// A continuation in the middle: the newline and its leading indent-free continuation
// collapse away, leaving exactly the two runs joined.
const s = "ab\
 cd"
print(s.length)
print(s)

// Joined with no gap — proves the newline itself is dropped rather than turned into a space.
const t = "ab\
cd"
print(t.length)
print(t)

// A continuation alongside an ORDINARY escape, so the two paths are exercised in one
// lexeme: `\t` must still produce a tab while `\<newline>` produces nothing at all.
const u = "a\tb\
 cd"
print(u.length)
// The tab and the space, by code point — asserting the rendered string here would need a
// literal tab inside an `@log` comment, which is exactly the kind of invisible-whitespace
// expectation that rots. `u` is `a`,TAB,`b`,SPACE,`c`,`d`: the escape survived as 9 and the
// continuation left the following space untouched at 32.
print(u[1])
print(u[3])
